WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.800
Good morning John.

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.820
Last week, I went to my first ever San Diego Comic Con.

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:06.880
I was there to do some panels, by myself and with some friends,

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.420
and also do a little performance.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:10.760
I had a lot of thoughts in this place,

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.860
about how weird it is that I get to be on the same stage as people I consider super legitimately famous.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:20.840
about how we create tiers of humanity and place extra weight on being part of one tier when

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:27.260
in fact I can solidly confirm that it is pretty identical to all the other tiers. About how conventions like this

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:32.080
and the ones that I help run serve to concentrate the reality of fandom and excitement and expression

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:38.440
into the real world and how great that is. But while at comic-con, I had a more abstract thought that hit me

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:47.000
pretty hard: there is so much media now! Oh my god, how do we consume it all?

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.620
When I got back home from comic-con, all my friends we talking about Stranger Things,

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:55.880
and I was like, "but, guy- I'm still catching up on Mr. Robot, I haven't even seen the new ghostbusters movie

00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:00.700
yet. There's so much music, so many books, so many comics, so many shows and movies and podcasts

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:06.740
and youtube videos. And we are all constantly bombarded by voices telling us to eat these things up

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:12.640
real quick and as soon as you're done, move on to the next one. The media industry advestises on its own

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:20.040
media to push yet more media, whether that's reality TV, sitcoms, dramas, or the news. Never before has there

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:27.780
been anything like this much media. But John, I've got a secret for you. The special thing about comic-con isn't

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:33.800
the amount of media these people consume, it's the passion they have for it. And in a world where you

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:39.720
nibble and snack and then move on to the next thing, that passion, which is the source of so much joy in my

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:47.020
life, doesn't get developed. And so I asked myself, what are the shows, the books, the podcasts, the online

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:53.300
communities that I spend more time thinking about and contributing to than consuming? What are the pieces of

00:01:53.360 --> 00:02:00.360
media that I go to over and over again, the albums that I can't stop listening to? The worlds I read fanfic in

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:05.960
because I don't want to stop being inside of them? The books I can read for a fourth time and still learn new

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:12.220
things about? The aren't that many. But I want there to be more. John, outside of my hotel room at comic-con,

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:18.260
there was a port for Dole produce. All of the shipping containers came off a boat, and then they got stored

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:23.040
and driven out by semi-trucks. And since this is a thoughts from places video, you might think I'm about

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:29.260
to make some weird, abstract connection between the stuff I was just talking about and this Dole shipping port

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:34.040
but that's just too much of a stretch, it's just that I wanted to show you this really cool timelapse

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:41.460
that I made. Anyway, I wanna snack less. And have more good, solid content meals. I wanna stop consuming

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:47.480
cheeto media and focus on the things in this world that make me love them. That take up my brain and make me

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:54.400
think for weeks afterward, that make me love the world and appreciate myself. I wanna love things the way that

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.960
these people love things. John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:03.180
Hey. If you wanna love things like that too, I might suggest that you check out nerdcon stories, which is

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:09.600
an amazing opportunity to love great stuff. I might be a little bit biased because it is a conference that I literally

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.660
built with a team of really great people, but last year it was just a fantastic time and I'm very sad to say that

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:19.760
I won't be able to attend this year for reasons that will become clear, especially if you listen to next week's Dear

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.240
Hank and John, or if you check out a very long video  that I just uploaded on hankschannel.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:28.520
So I won't be there, but John will, and so will a bunch of other amazing people like John Scalzi, Mary Robinette

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:33.360
Kowal, Dylan Marron, Ben Acker and Ben Blacker, Cindy Pon, M. T. Anderson, John Darnielle of The Mountain

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.660
Goats, Dessa Darling, Maureen Johnson, Nalo Hopkinson, Patrick Rothfuss, Paolo Bacigalupi,

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:42.480
a ton of other great people, it's gonna be a wonderful time. I'm really sad that I can't be there but I hope that

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.640
you all have a great time without me. If you want to find out more, you can go to nerdcon.com.

